<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="pp-1">
  <title>Portfolio Problem 1</title>

  <introduction>
	<theorem>
		<statement>
			<p>If <m>n</m> is an even integer, then <m>n^{2} + 8n + 9</m> is an odd integer.</p>
		</statement>
	</theorem>
  </introduction>

  <!-- Can also be <handout> instead of <subsection> to get a printable version -->

  <subsection>
  	<title>Instructions</title>
	
	<ol>
		<li><p>Complete all specifications for the draft, below. Then wait for feedback.</p></li>
		<li><p>Once you've reached <em>math successful</em> on the draft, complete all specifications for the writing revision, below. Then wait for more feedback!</p></li>
		<li><p>After the writing revision, you can revise once more and submit a <em>final revision</em> by the final exam. Only this final revision will count towards your final grade.</p></li>
	</ol>
  </subsection>
  
  <subsection>
  	<title>Specifications for the draft</title>
	<p>To earn <em>math successful</em>, do all of these:</p>
	<ol>
		<li><p>Submit a draft proof of the theorem. A know-show table is good, but you don't have to use that format. Include all key mathematical ideas and work so that you have a complete solution. Include just enough writing and detail to make it understandable. Drafts can be hand-written -- don't focus on communication specifications nor typing them up yet.</p></li>
		<li><p><em>Try and include examples:</em> Try a few concrete examples of the theorem. Substitute in a variety of appropriate values for <m>n</m> to see how this theorem works. Include at least 3 examples in your draft and writing revision. For each, show how you know the hypothesis and conclusion are true (see the <url href="https://www.overleaf.com/read/qndbyzmrxprd">Sample Portfolio Solution</url> for an example of how to format these).</p></li>
	</ol>
  </subsection>
  
  <subsection>
  	<title>Specifications for the writing revision</title>
	<p>To earn <em>math &amp; writing successful</em>, do all of these:</p>
	<ol>
		<li><p>First complete everything for the draft, above</p></li>
		<li><p>Type your work in <latex />. Find a template, how-to guide, and sample portfolio solution on Blackboard.</p></li>
		<li><p>Upload a PDF to Blackboard and include a <q>view only</q> link to your Overleaf document in the text field.</p></li>
		<li><p>Make sure your writing follows <em>all</em> of the conventions listed below (click each one to get more details and examples):</p></li>
	</ol>
	
	<p><xref ref="wc-statement" text="type-local-title" /></p>
	
	<p><xref ref="wc-assumptions" text="type-local-title" /></p>
	
	<p><xref ref="wc-proof-method" text="type-local-title" /></p>
	
	<p><xref ref="wc-justify" text="type-local-title" /></p>
		
	<p><xref ref="wc-variables" text="type-local-title" /></p>
	
	<p><xref ref="wc-definitions" text="type-local-title" /></p>
	
